{"instructions": "Explain how a university registration system should handle two students simultaneously requesting the last seat. Discuss transactions, duplicate prevention, and the benefits and risks of caching. This is a theoretical answer, not a coding task.", "criterion": "Distinguish duplicate enrollment from capacity concurrency", "rubric": {"policy": "Assess theoretical understanding. A valid conceptual explanation suffices; do not require SQL code, every listed alternative, or topics outside the selected criterion. Separate independent criteria. An explicitly false claim is incorrect, not just missing coverage.", "criterion": "Distinguish duplicate enrollment from capacity concurrency", "professional_principle": "Duplicate enrollment means the same student enrolling in the same course twice. Capacity concurrency means different students competing for limited seats. A UNIQUE key on the pair (student, course) rejects a repeated pair; different students form different pairs, so this key alone cannot enforce a course-wide capacity bound.", "valid_solutions": ["Explicitly distinguish the per-student duplicate key from a separate shared-capacity reservation rule.", "Explain that duplicate prevention and last-seat concurrency need distinct protections, even when both are used inside a transaction."], "scope": "Judge the actual meaning of a claimed guarantee, not just the presence of UNIQUE or transaction keywords. A claim that a student/course uniqueness key prevents different students taking the final seat is technically incorrect."}, "submission": "I would add a UNIQUE constraint on student ID and course ID. This guarantees that two different students cannot take the last seat. A transaction can update the seat count and save the enrollment together, with rollback if either fails. A cache makes reads faster. If the cache says a seat is available, we can confirm enrollment immediately without a database check."}
